A||||9||Cheever2||Considering A/C for garage||||1||
Z||000000||Cheever2||05-09-2001||02:27 PM||||Many forum members were not able to work on their car during winter.  I didn't laugh, knowing my turn will come this summer.<P>I've got a detached 2-car garage, interior dimensions 20 x 21 (420 sq ft).  It is not insulated, and has no interior ceiling.  I'm thinking of putting in a window air conditioner (have no window - will have to make an opening).  Lowe's has a Whirlpool 10,200 BTU which they say cools up to 500 sq ft, for $279 US.  For $50 more they have one with 11,600 BTU.  The next size up is more than I want to spend.<P>My questions are: (1) Would it all be wasted due to the garage being uninsulated; and (2) What should it cost to have an electrician get the wiring ready?  My circuit breaker box is right there next to the workbench.<P>Also, locating the a/c next to the workbench would be nice for standing in the cool blowing air, but the noise would make conversations difficult.  So maybe it should be in a far corner.  Then again, I don't usually have company while I'm out there.  Those with advice are invited for cold beverages when the job is done.  Even those without advice.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||05-09-2001||02:57 PM||||if i were you i would get the 11,600 one, just so it will bring it cold faster then you can turn it down once it gewts as cold as you want<P>about the insolation...  is there any open areas you can fill with expanding foam?  I would atleast try to insolate under the floor (if it is not cement) and the celing/roof...    you will save youself alot of money on electricity if you insolate the whole thing though<P>for electrical most A/C's are 110 volts so you can just run a regular extention coard to it OR put a electrical box (and outlet) next to where the A/C is going to go in then run a wire to the nearest outlet OR main braker you will see in it where to put the wires to, even the outlets have a mark of what color goes where...     I remember when i rewired my shed i did:<BR>White - Positve<BR>Green - Ground<BR>Black - Negative<BR>BUT  my dad then told me Black was positve in Alternating current systems unlike black is negative in direct current systems (aka. car)<BR>not sure if that is true though... just trying to help and/or give ideas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||05-09-2001||04:23 PM||||Get the big one. On days that you know that you will be out in the garage, turn it on early before the temperature has time to come up in the building. Once everything is hot, it is pretty hard to get it comfortable again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-09-2001||05:14 PM||||Do the electrical yourself. I'm a big electricity wuss (paranoid about getting shocked) and wired up the 220 for my big air compressor. The breakers snap in, just keep your fingers and metal tools off the bus bars in the back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. If you can do-it-yourself on your Fiero, you will find that home wiring is easy.<P>Two other things you oughta think about:<BR>1. Get a big attic fan and mount it up high in the back wall. You would be amazed at the difference air movement will make.<BR>2. get some single faced insulation and a staple gun when you pick up your window a/c unit. When you get the a/c, buy all the BTU's you can afford. You might even look in the nickel trader or flea markets to find a used a/c unit that might be ugly but good enough for a shop.||||reg||10
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-09-2001||05:26 PM||||I wish my shop was ACd. but with all the sanding, body filler dust, and paint, Id clog up the filter every day or two. If i just did mechanical work, def have AC. Ya, get the biggest you can. cheaper in long run to run a big one for a short time, than a little one running all day. The bigger will cycle itself on and off, the little one would prob just run on full all day.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||05-09-2001||06:11 PM||||Somebody might shoot me for saying this, but..<P>I insulated my garage and created a ceiling in there, by using 2x4' pieces of styrofoam.  You make them out of 4x8' sheets.  The ones I used were about an inch thick.  Tacked them to the ceiling joists (if that's what they're called?) with sheet rock screws, with used, washed can lids under the heads to prevent pull-through.  The lids were from the sack lunch I brought to work every day.<P>Total cost: about $100, for a large (4-Fiero) garage.  Plus you're recycling can lids, if that's a plus.  It's a project that you work on when you feel like it.  Eventually the entire garage gets an insulated ceiling.  Another nicety is that the white foam panels distribute light rather well, and make the garage look, well, cheerier.<P>I can't believe I confessed to doing this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Oh, well, it's good for the soul  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, they say!         ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||05-09-2001||06:25 PM||||We just finished building an 8 car garage / shop at my house , it's insulated and has Both AC and Gas heat. The thing is that even over the weekend the garage was cool in 90- degree weather. IT has a high pitched roof with black shingles and is all brick. I was amazed that the garage was cool. Insulate the garage then see how it holds / reflects heat.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||05-09-2001||07:05 PM||||Do as much insulating as you can.It will pay for itself in money saved and additional comfort.I have a 26'x30' garage,it is well insulated and I heat it with a small ceramic heater during the winter.I have a small window unit I use if it gets hot.As far as the size of the air conditioner,you should get the smallest unit that will do the job.If you get one that is too big,it will reach temperature too fast and not remove the moisture from the air.Have you ever been in a room that was cool,but felt muggy?The air conditioner was too big.You are better off with one that will run longer and remove the moisture. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-09-2001||09:19 PM||||I'm assuming we're talking box with a peak roof..... No ceiling at all....<P>Get some thing and make a ceiling. Something cheap that won't sag allover. Throw some insulation on top of this if you can.<P>In the space above that, install a vent so heat from the roof can get out.<P>These 2 things alone will make the space better to work in. Will also make winter heating work better if need be for those in cold places.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||05-10-2001||05:31 PM||||I'll give you my perspective as a guy who spent several years selling HVAC equipment, and then several more as a general contractor building garages and room additions. If you aren't going to insulate, don't baother with the A/C, it'll never keep up. In a space like that without insulation, probably nothing short of two or three tons cooling capacity with an equal amount of airflow would ever turn off. The attic space superheats, and sheds heat into the lower areas, even hours after the sun goes down. (That's why upstairs bedrooms even in houses with good insulation are like ovens late in the evening)<BR>You can always put insulation in the rafter spaces next to the roof sheathing so as not to lose the storage space of what would be the cieling joists. A load calculation would show that insulation in the walls would help, but the single greatest heat loss/ gain is from the ceiling. <BR>The truth is, a couple of windows, and the attic fan mentioned would be a GREAT alternative to the A/C and would probably keep it cooler. Just make sure you have sufficient gable or hat vents to let the hot air escape.<BR>If you absolutely have to go with the A/C, then insulation is a must. The idea about styrofoam is agood idea. And they sell nails and screws with plastic washers so you don't have to save a years worth of can lids to attach it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> There are several types of styrofoam insulation available, all with various R ratings. And if you don't like the fiberglass itch, Owens makes an insulation that's completely encased in a plastic sheath. It's itch-free, but pricey.<BR>Good luck!<BR>~Taiji<BR>Oh yeah, BTW, when they advertise 500 sq fee5t, it's an assumed 8 foot insulated ceiling, which translates into 4,000 cu/ft. Assuming you have a 6/12 pitch on your roof, you are actually trying to condition 4,400 cu/ft of UNinsulated space. <BR>Ciao....<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
